# Research

## Guava and Post-Harvest Loss

Guava is a highly perishable climacteric fruit. During storage and transportation, ripening continues due to ethylene production and respiration. Mechanical damage and excess moisture can further contribute to quality deterioration.

## Ethylene and Ripening

Guava can produce roughly **1–20 µL of ethylene per kilogram per hour at 20°C**, depending on its variety and maturity. In one reported study, ethylene production reached approximately **12.9 µL/kg/hour** during peak ripening.

Therefore, the Guava Guard liner aims to reduce the accumulation of ethylene around the fruit and thereby slow down the ripening process.

Guava Guard proposes the use of an **ethylene-scavenging layer** to reduce ethylene accumulation in the package environment.

## Rice Husk Biochar

Rice husk is an abundant agricultural residue. Through pyrolysis, it can be converted into biochar.

The proposed Guava Guard design uses **rice husk biochar as an ethylene-scavenging material**. Further experimental testing is required to determine its actual adsorption capacity and optimum quantity for the liner.

## Cellulose-Based Material

A porous cellulose-based layer is proposed as a protective interface between the scavenging material and the fruit.

The porous structure is intended to allow gas movement while providing mechanical support and helping manage excess moisture.

## Banana Pseudostem Porous Layer Preparation

### Raw Material – Banana Pseudostem

Banana pseudostem is selected as the raw material for the porous protective layer. It is an abundant agricultural residue containing cellulose-rich fibres. Its natural fibrous structure makes it suitable for developing a lightweight and porous sheet.

### Alkali Treatment Using NaOH

The banana pseudostem is subjected to **alkali treatment using sodium hydroxide (NaOH)**. This treatment helps break down and remove part of the **lignin and hemicellulose** present in the plant cell-wall structure, exposing the cellulose-rich fibres.

This treatment improves fibre separation and makes the material more suitable for forming a porous sheet.

### Preparation of Banana Pseudostem Fibre Layer

After alkali treatment, the banana pseudostem material is processed to obtain the separated fibres. These fibres are arranged and pressed to form a **thin, porous sheet**.

The porous structure is intended to permit gas movement and assist in moisture management while providing a physical barrier between the fruit and the biochar layer.

### Preparation of Tapioca Starch Adhesive

**Tapioca starch** is used as a natural adhesive for bonding the two layers. A starch paste is prepared and applied between the banana pseudostem layer and the rice husk biochar layer.

The use of tapioca starch provides a simple, low-cost and bio-based bonding method.

### Layer Assembly

The two main layers are assembled:

**Layer 1 – Rice Husk Biochar**

Proposed to act as the **ethylene-scavenging layer**.

**Layer 2 – Banana Pseudostem Fibre**

Acts as the **porous protective and moisture-managing layer** and prevents direct contact between the biochar and the fruit.

Tapioca starch paste is applied between the two layers to provide adhesion.

### Pressing and Drying

After applying the tapioca starch paste, the two layers are placed together and subjected to **manual pressure** to improve contact and bonding.

The assembled structure is then **dried** to remove excess moisture and allow the starch adhesive to form a stronger bond.

### Final Guava Guard Liner

After drying, the bonded two-layer structure forms the proposed **Guava Guard liner**.

The final liner consists of:

**Rice Husk Biochar Layer → Tapioca Starch Adhesive → Banana Pseudostem Fibre Layer**

The biochar layer is positioned away from direct fruit contact, while the banana pseudostem layer acts as the protective interface.

### Functional Role of Each Layer

| Layer | Material | Main Function |
|---|---|---|
| Layer 1 | Rice Husk Biochar | Proposed ethylene scavenging |
| Adhesive | Tapioca Starch | Bonds the two layers |
| Layer 2 | Banana Pseudostem Fibre | Porous protection and moisture management |

The complete structure is designed as a **low-cost, agricultural-waste-based liner for guava storage and transportation**.

## Research Gap

Existing ethylene-absorbing technologies are available, but Guava Guard focuses on integrating an **agricultural-waste-based scavenging layer** with a **banana pseudostem fibre layer** into a simple liner format that can fit existing fruit crates without electricity or complicated equipment.

Further experimental studies are required to validate **ethylene adsorption, moisture management, mechanical strength and actual shelf-life extension**.
